esp32c3: the USB-Serial-JTAG's serial port, as a console

Boards without a USB-UART chip, like the ESP32-C3 SuperMini, put Arduino's
Serial on the C3's USB-Serial-JTAG (USB CDC on boot). lcgamboa's and
Espressif's models of it are empty stubs, so in the simulator those
sketches ran silently: 41 of the library's builds.

This models the serial (CDC-ACM) side. Bytes written to EP1 go to the
device's chardev when the firmware flushes (WR_DONE) or the 64-byte FIFO
fills (a host drains it without waiting), raising SERIAL_IN_EMPTY; the IN
side always reports free space. Bytes from the chardev arrive on EP1's OUT
side with SERIAL_OUT_RECV_PKT. The interrupt (INT_RAW/ST/ENA/CLR) goes to
the interrupt matrix, and the USB frame counter (FRAM_NUM) advances with
virtual time, as a host's start-of-frame would, so ESP-IDF's connection
monitor and Arduino's HWCDC see a host attached. JTAG is not emulated.

The chardev is the device's "chardev" property, set with
-global driver=misc.esp32c3.usb_serial_jtag,property=chardev,value=<id>.
Its handlers use the fork's own main context (g_main_context_default_l),
as the fork's UARTs do: with the default context QEMU never accepted the
chardev's socket.

esp32bench enables it only for boards whose Serial is on USB: with it
wired, about one IDF boot in five on the C3 lost its UART0 output partway
through (cause not found; see docs/qemu-forks.md).

Written for esp32bench (GPL-2.0-or-later), changing Espressif's
hw/misc/esp32c3_jtag.c and its header (Copyright (c) 2023 Espressif
Systems (Shanghai) Co. Ltd., GPL-2.0-or-later, notice kept) and
lcgamboa's hw/riscv/esp32c3_picsimlab.c. Applied by esp32bench
(core/crates/sim/src/lcgamboa.rs) to lcgamboa/qemu at
885a6e7061fb70fac78a074a719423810bc19a17, after patch 0005.

